    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d579">

                <pb facs="0790" n="714" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg714"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">651.5231/151: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-12T14:00:00+00:00">July 12, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 12—12:36 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>47. My telegram No. 46, July 10, 2 p.m.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d579-f1"/> French
                    products now not only in the first column but have been subjected to duty
                    surcharge of over 41%. This accomplished by means of decree March 10, 1934,
                    authorizing Government to increase tariff rates in case of dumping decree, cites
                    currency depreciation as a form of dumping.</p>
                <p>If our treaty negotiations fail my personal opinion is same measures may well be
                    taken against us which in conjunction with lack of quotas would result in
                    depriving us of most of exports to Spain which amounted last year to over
                    $38,000,000.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d579-f1" n="15" xml:id="d579-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the Spanish-French negotiations had been broken off
                    (651.5231/150).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
